## How `:name` parameters are captured

There are two phases: **building** the wildcard node at registration time, and **extracting** the value at match time.

### 1. Registration — creating a `param` node (`tree.go:288` `insertChild`)

When a route like `/users/:name` is registered, `insertChild` scans the path with `findWildcard` (tree.go:253), which looks for a `:` or `*` and returns the wildcard token (e.g. `:name`) up to the next `/`.

For a `:` wildcard (tree.go:311-336):
```go
if wildcard[0] == ':' { // param
    if i > 0 {
        n.path = path[:i]       // split off the static prefix before ':'
        path = path[i:]
    }
    child := &node{
        nType:    param,
        path:     wildcard,      // literally ":name", including the colon
        fullPath: fullPath,
    }
    n.addChild(child)
    n.wildChild = true           // marks the parent: "my special child is a wildcard"
    n = child
    ...
    if len(wildcard) < len(path) {
        // more path after the param (e.g. "/:name/profile") — recurse into a new child
        ...
        continue
    }
    n.handlers = handlers        // ":name" is the last segment — attach the handler here
    return
}
```
So the node's `path` field stores the raw `:name` (colon included), its `nType` is `param`, and the parent sets `wildChild = true` so the matcher knows this branch isn't a plain static-index lookup.

Registration also panics on conflicts — e.g. registering `/users/:id` and later `/users/:name` under the same parent triggers the wildcard-conflict panic in `addRoute` (tree.go:~217-235), since only one param child is allowed per node.

### 2. Matching — extracting the value (`tree.go:418` `getValue`, `param` case around line 483)

During lookup, once the static-prefix walk exhausts static `indices` and hits a node with `wildChild == true`, the matcher jumps to `n.children[len(n.children)-1]` (the param/catchAll child is always kept last) and, for `nType == param`:

```go
case param:
    // Find param end (either '/' or path end)
    end := 0
    for end < len(path) && path[end] != '/' {
        end++
    }
    if params != nil {
        ...
        val := path[:end]                    // the actual URL segment, e.g. "alice"
        if unescape {
            if v, err := url.QueryUnescape(val); err == nil { val = v }
        }
        (*value.params)[i] = Param{
            Key:   n.path[1:],                // strip the leading ':' → "name"
            Value: val,
        }
    }
    if end < len(path) {
        // more path remains after this segment — descend into n.children[0] and continue walking
        ...
    }
    if value.handlers = n.handlers; value.handlers != nil {
        return value
    }
```

Key points:
- The captured value is just the substring of the request path up to the next `/` (or end of string) — `path[:end]`.
- The key is derived by stripping the leading `:` off the stored node path (`n.path[1:]`).
- Results accumulate into a `Params` slice (`[]Param{Key, Value}`, defined at tree.go:17-24), pre-sized using `globalParamsCount`/`countParams` (computed once at `addRoute` time, gin.go:378-380) so no allocation happens during matching — this is the "zero allocation router" claim from the README.
- After `handleHTTPRequest` (gin.go:690) gets `value.params != nil`, it assigns `c.Params = *value.params`, which is what `c.Param("name")` (via `Params.ByName`, tree.go:40) reads in handlers.

Catch-all (`*filepath`) works the same way structurally but takes the rest of the path unconditionally (no `/`-boundary check) — that branch is later in `getValue` (past line 560), which I did not re-verify in this pass.

